  "summary": "{{p:1}} (Betty), the project manager, opened the meeting by asking the team to introduce their roles: {{p:2}} (Francina) is responsible for user-interface design of the remote control, {{p:4}} (Eileen) handles marketing and how to sell it, and {{p:3}} (Jeanne-Oui) is the industrial designer responsible for technical-functional design and specifications. Betty said she was the project manager for today’s meeting. The team reviewed the work process: functional, conceptual, and detailed design would each involve individual work followed by discussion; they also tried the whiteboard so they could use it in a future presentation. The new remote should be original, trendy, and user-friendly; the financial constraints are a selling price of €25, a company profit aim of €50 million, an international market, and a production cost below €12.50, with materials and cost considered during design. Initial design ideas included a compact, multipurpose remote that could control a TV and other household equipment such as audio players, air conditioning, and heating, as well as distinctive colors, materials, shapes, and a comfortable grip. The group also discussed helping users find a misplaced remote, including a light or beep triggered by a sensor when it is hidden or in darkness; a clap- or voice-activated signal was raised, but {{p:1}} felt those triggers were not specific enough, and the feature was left for later discussion. Before adjourning, {{p:1}} said the next meeting would be in 30 minutes and assigned {{p:3}} the working/technical-functional design, while {{p:4}} would develop user-requirement specifications, including friendliness and a justification for the set price through sufficient features; everyone would receive specific instructions.",
  "mom": "### Introductions and meeting plan\n{{p:1}} opened by asking everyone to introduce themselves and describe their functions. {{p:2}} introduced herself as Francina, responsible for user interface work and designing a television remote control. {{p:4}} introduced herself as Eileen, working in marketing and focused on how to sell the product. {{p:3}} introduced herself as Jeanne-Oui, an industrial designer responsible for the technical-functional designs and specifications of the user interface and for working on its design. {{p:1}} introduced herself as Betty, the project manager for today’s meeting. She proposed reviewing the presentation to clarify the assignment. She said the opening and acquaintance were underway, that the computer appeared to be the tool, and that each person would receive instructions. The project plan fell under the same heading, and she did not expect a major discussion at that point.\n\n### Remote-control design brief and work stages\n{{p:1}} said the team would design a new remote control that should be original, trendy, and user-friendly, and asked everyone to note those requirements. She explained that functional, conceptual, and detailed design would each involve individual work followed by discussion at a meeting. {{p:4}} summarized these as three types of design: functional, conceptual, and detailed. During the discussion, {{p:1}} remarked that the writing implement was difficult to write with, and {{p:4}} joked that they could redesign it after finishing the remote control.\n\n### Whiteboard tool practice\n{{p:1}} proposed trying the whiteboard as tool training, with participants drawing a favorite animal so they would know how to use it if they needed to present something on it at the next meeting. She invited {{p:4}} to try it; {{p:4}} agreed to see what she could do and drew a cat, noting that she had a very fat cat that liked to sit in that position. {{p:1}} reminded her to press the control so the drawing would record. {{p:1}} then invited {{p:2}} to try the board, and {{p:2}} agreed and asked what to draw. {{p:3}} suggested a snake, which {{p:2}} drew. {{p:4}} hoped the cat would be hungry because she did not like snakes. {{p:1}} said they could discuss erasing the drawing later.\n\n### Financial and market constraints\n{{p:1}} presented the project finance figures: the selling price was to be 25 euros; the company’s profit aim was 50 million euros; the market range was unlimited, meaning international; and production cost should hopefully be less than 12.50 euros and must not exceed that amount. {{p:4}} observed that the targets would keep everyone challenged. {{p:1}} said the team should keep the budget in mind when choosing materials and designing the product.\n\n### Initial ideas for the remote control\n{{p:1}} asked whether anyone had experience using remote controls and invited ideas about what users disliked or would want changed, including size and button quantity or legibility. {{p:2}} said remotes should be compact, noting that current ones were very long, and suggested a multipurpose device that could control televisions, audio players, household temperature, air conditioners, and heating systems. {{p:1}} restated the concept as a multifunctional gadget for household appliances, and the others agreed. {{p:4}} said that, to market successfully, the product should do more things; {{p:1}} added that it should offer something new and different from existing remotes. The group also considered making it trendy and distinctive through different colors, materials, and shapes. {{p:1}} suggested a kidney-shaped form that might feel better in the hand, and {{p:4}} agreed that a friendly shape would help.\n\n### Finding a misplaced remote\n{{p:4}} suggested that the remote could beep when someone claps, because people often lose it and cannot find it. {{p:1}} agreed that remotes can be covered by a newspaper or hidden behind a plant, especially when someone wants to turn off the television and cannot locate it. {{p:1}}, {{p:3}}, and {{p:4}} discussed a sound or signal, such as a beep or blinking light, to help locate it. {{p:3}} said a light could make its location recognizable. In response to {{p:4}} asking what should trigger the signal, {{p:1}} proposed a light sensor that would activate when the remote was hidden in a dark spot, such as beneath a newspaper or sweater or behind a plant, and then emit a light signal or beep. {{p:4}} confirmed the idea as a light sensor activating the signal. {{p:1}} considered voice or clapping insufficiently specific, noting that clap-operated lamps only work to a certain degree. {{p:4}} pointed out that a remote might be in an obvious place and still go unnoticed, for example on top of a bookcase when it is usually kept on a coffee table. {{p:1}} said they had good initial points but needed to move along.\n\n### Assignments and next meeting\n{{p:1}} said the next meeting would be in 30 minutes and checked whether everyone had received the computer notices; {{p:2}} and {{p:3}} confirmed receipt. The team clarified the design responsibilities: {{p:3}} would work on the technical-functional or working design, while {{p:2}} would produce the functional design. {{p:1}} assigned {{p:4}} the user-requirements specification, including friendliness and the general ideas discussed, as well as consideration of price. {{p:4}} said the team would need to justify the given price by providing sufficient features to make the product sell at that price, and {{p:1}} agreed. {{p:1}} said they would receive specific instructions, asked for questions or suggestions, and {{p:4}} said she had enough to think about until the next meeting. {{p:1}} closed by saying they would meet in about 30 minutes and see what they could develop.",
